## Valid Shapes by Construction

Every rectangle must have positive width and height. No exceptions. Encode this once as invariants and every function that touches a Rectangle is guaranteed to work with a legitimate shape:

```xiom
type Rectangle = {
  width: Float64;
  height: Float64;
  invariant: width > 0.0;
  invariant: height > 0.0;
}

fn Rectangle.area(self: &Self) -> Float64
  ensures: result > 0.0;
{
  return self.width * self.height;
}

fn Rectangle.perimeter(self: &Self) -> Float64
  ensures: result > 0.0;
{
  return 2.0 * (self.width + self.height);
}

fn Rectangle.is_square(self: &Self) -> Bool {
  return self.width == self.height;
}
```

Notice how `area` and `perimeter` have `ensures: result > 0.0` — this is only provable because the invariants guarantee positive dimensions. Without invariants, you couldn't trust that `area > 0`.

### Safe Scaling
```xiom
fn Rectangle.scale(self: &mut Self, factor: Float64)
  requires: factor > 0.0;
  ensures: self.area() == self@pre.area() * factor * factor;
{
  self.width = self.width * factor;
  self.height = self.height * factor;
}
```

The `requires` ensures scaling doesn't accidentally zero-out or invert the shape. The `ensures` proves the area scales by `factor²` — which is geometrically correct.

